******************************************************************1725 1451  124
ORNL Resonance Parameter Evaluation, October, 2003.               1725 1451  125
R. O. Sayer, K. H. Guber, L. C. Leal, N. M. Larson, T. Rauscher   1725 1451  126
                                                                  1725 1451  127
Our resonance parameter evaluation is described below.   File 3   1725 1451  128
total [MT 1], elastic [MT 2], and capture [MT 102] cross section  1725 1451  129
values for T=0K for  were computed from the resonance parameter   1725 1451  130
representation.   For the energy range 0.00001 eV to 1.2 MeV, the 1725 1451  131
pointwise File 3 cross sections are based on the resonance        1725 1451  132
parameter files.  Above 1.2 MeV, the current (February 2000) ENDF 1725 1451  133
cross sections are used.  The CL35 file also contains (n,p) [MT   1725 1451  134
103,600] cross sections for T = 0K.  For the energy range 0.00001 1725 1451  135
eV to 121 keV, the pointwise cross sections are based on the      1725 1451  136
resonance parameter file.   Above 121 keV, the current (February  1725 1451  137
2000) ENDF cross sections are used.   The last resonance with     1725 1451  138
non-zero proton width is at 103.5 keV.                            1725 1451  139
                                                                  1725 1451  140
We performed an evaluation [1] of Cl neutron cross sections in    1725 1451  141
the resolved resonance region with the multilevel Reich-Moore     1725 1451  142
R-matrix formalism. Resonance analyses were carried out with the  1725 1451  143
computer code SAMMY [3], which utilizes Bayes' method, a          1725 1451  144
generalized least squares technique.  A recent modification of    1725 1451  145
SAMMY enabled us to calculate charged particle penetrabilities    1725 1451  146
for the proton exit channel.  Our evaluation takes advantage of   1725 1451  147
recent high-resolution capture and transmission measurements at   1725 1451  148
the Oak Ridge Electron Linear Accelerator (ORELA) to extend the   1725 1451  149
resolved resonance energy range to 1.2 MeV with much more         1725 1451  150
accurate representation of the data than previous evaluations.    1725 1451  151
                                                                  1725 1451  152
The total cross section data include measurements by Guber, et    1725 1451  153
al. [2] and Good, et al. [4], on the 80-m flight path at ORELA;   1725 1451  154
Cierjacks, et al. [5], on a 57-m flight path at the Karlsruhe     1725 1451  155
Isochronous Cyclotron; Singh, et al. [6] on the 200-m flight      1725 1451  156
path at the Columbia synchrocyclotron; Brugger, et al. [7], who   1725 1451  157
utilized a crystal spectrometer and also the MTR fast chopper     1725 1451  158
with a flight path of 45 m; Kiehn, et al. [8], with the           1725 1451  159
Rockefeller generator; and Newson, et al. [9], at the Duke Van    1725 1451  160
de Graaff facility.                                               1725 1451  161
                                                                  1725 1451  162
Also included in the evaluation were the high resolution capture  1725 1451  163
cross section data (0.1 < En < 500 keV) of Guber, et al. [2] and  1725 1451  164
the older, low resolution capture data (0.02 < En < 1.0 keV) of   1725 1451  165
Kashukeev, et al. [10]. The 35Cl(n,p)35S cross section data of    1725 1451  166
Koehler [11] and Druyts, et al. [12] were also fit. The proton    1725 1451  167
widths are significant fractions of the total widths for          1725 1451  168
resonances at 398 and 4251 eV.                                    1725 1451  169
                                                                  1725 1451  170
In order to give a proper treatment for charged particles in an   1725 1451  171
exit channel, an algorithm to calculate charged particle          1725 1451  172
penetrabilities (CPP) and shifts was incorporated in the SAMMY    1725 1451  173
code. The methodology for CPP computation has been given          1725 1451  174
previously [13].  Routines based on the CPP algorithm have been   1725 1451  175
developed and incorporated in a development version of the        1725 1451  176
nuclear data processing code NJOY [14] for use in preparing data  1725 1451  177
for criticality safety benchmark calculations.  The nuclear       1725 1451  178
radii used for penetrabilities and shifts were computed           1725 1451  179
according to   R = 1.23A**0.33333 + 0.8 fm, where A is the        1725 1451  180
nuclide mass. These values were 4.8222 and 4.8974 fm for 35Cl     1725 1451  181
and 37Cl, respectively. In the SAMMY analysis the radii used to   1725 1451  182
compute hard sphere phase shifts were allowed to vary, and        1725 1451  183
different radii were allowed for s- and p-waves.  Final values    1725 1451  184
for 35Cl were R(L=0,1) = 3.6680, 4.8888 fm; final values for 37Cl 1725 1451  185
were R(L=0,1) = 3.3651,3.9565 fm.                                 1725 1451  186
                                                                  1725 1451  187
Capture Cross Section Analysis                                    1725 1451  188
------------------------------                                    1725 1451  189
Guber, et al. [2] measured the neutron capture of chlorine up to  1725 1451  190
500 keV using a natural LiCl sample of thickness 0.09812 atoms/b  1725 1451  191
and the ORELA capture system, which had been re-engineered [20]   1725 1451  192
to minimize the amount of structural material surrounding the     1725 1451  193
sample and detectors. To calculate accurate correction factors    1725 1451  194
for experimental effects of the neutron capture data, reliable    1725 1451  195
neutron widths are needed since the sample was fairly thick.      1725 1451  196
Initial neutron widths were obtained by  fitting the              1725 1451  197
transmission data; using these newly determined values,           1725 1451  198
corrections for self-shielding and multiple scattering were       1725 1451  199
calculated with SAMMY and used to determine capture widths.       1725 1451  200
Several iterations of fitting the transmission and capture data   1725 1451  201
were performed to obtain final resonance parameters for 0.1 < En  1725 1451  202
< 500 keV.   From their resonance parameters, Guber, et al. [2],  1725 1451  203
calculated average cross sections that were rather different      1725 1451  204
from ENDF/B-VI. This difference is very likely the result of      1725 1451  205
underestimated neutron sensitivity in the older measurements as   1725 1451  206
well as an improved calculation of the weighting function.        1725 1451  207
                                                                  1725 1451  208
In nuclides where the (n,gamma) cross section is small, the       1725 1451  209
direct capture (DC) is often a significant fraction of the cross  1725 1451  210
section. Guber, et al. [2] describe in detail the DC              1725 1451  211
calculations they performed for 35Cl and 37Cl using the code      1725 1451  212
TEDCA [21, 22]. They calculated that the effect of the DC         1725 1451  213
component was very small for 35Cl where the thermal capture       1725 1451  214
cross section is 43.6 b. However, for 37Cl approximately 0.31 b   1725 1451  215
of the thermal capture cross section of 0.433 b is due to direct  1725 1451  216
capture. We have deduced a set of resonance parameters,           1725 1451  217
including the external level parameters, that reproduce the       1725 1451  218
resonant part of the capture cross section. To this resonant      1725 1451  219
part, one must add the DC contribution to obtain the overall      1725 1451  220
capture cross section. The thermal value of the DC cross section  1725 1451  221
is 0.16 +- 0.05 b for 35Cl and 0.31 +- 0.16 b for 37Cl.           1725 1451  222
                                                                  1725 1451  223
Thermal and Integral Quantities                                   1725 1451  224
-------------------------------                                   1725 1451  225
The following table gives a comparison of our elastic, capture,   1725 1451  226
(n,p), and total cross sections for En = 0.0253 eV and T = 0K     1725 1451  227
with the corresponding ENDF/B-VI quantities, which are based      1725 1451  228
principally on the compilation of Mughabghab [15]. The capture    1725 1451  229
cross section values include the DC contribution.  Also given is  1725 1451  230
the resonance capture integral, I-gamma.                          1725 1451  231
