NEUTRON ENERGIES ABOVE 226 keV                                    1725 1451  360
                                                                  1725 1451  361
 SUMMARY                                                          1725 1451  362
   This evaluation is based primarily on a theoretical analysis   1725 1451  363
 between neutron energies of 100 keV and 20 MeV that is optimized 1725 1451  364
 to the somewhat limited available experimental data.  Elastic    1725 1451  365
 and inelastic scattering angular distributions (through MT=70)   1725 1451  366
 are taken from the JENDL3.2 evaluation [Wa94]. Also, the MF3,    1725 1451  367
 MT102 data of JENDL below 4 MeV were adopted.                    1725 1451  368
   The theoretical calculations utilize Hauser-Feshbach           1725 1451  369
 statistical theory with corrections for width fluctuations,      1725 1451  370
 preequilibrium and direct reaction processes.  Spherical optical 1725 1451  371
 model calculations are used to obtain the neutron total cross    1725 1451  372
 section and neutron, proton, deuteron, triton, and alpha         1725 1451  373
 transmission coefficients.                                       1725 1451  374
   Cross sections and spectra for individual reactions are        1725 1451  375
 included for exiting neutron, proton, deuteron, alpha, and       1725 1451  376
 gamma-ray reactions.  Multiplicities, angular distributions, and 1725 1451  377
 emission energy spectra are given for gamma rays, particles, and 1725 1451  378
 recoil nuclei emitted in the dominant reactions, utilizing File  1725 1451  379
 6 of the ENDF/B-6 format [Ro91].  Energy-angle-correlated        1725 1451  380
 spectra are given for all outgoing particles and photons, and    1725 1451  381
 residual nuclei energy distributions are included.               1725 1451  382
                                                                  1725 1451  383
 THEORY                                                           1725 1451  384
                                                                  1725 1451  385
   HAUSER-FESHBACH STATISTICAL THEORY CALCULATIONS.  The GNASH    1725 1451  386
 code [Yo92] was used for all Hauser-Feshbach statistical theory  1725 1451  387
 calculations.  Preequilibrium corrections were performed in the  1725 1451  388
 course of the GNASH calculations using the exciton model of      1725 1451  389
 Kalbach [Ka77,Ka85].  Discrete level data from nuclear data      1725 1451  390
 sheets were matched to continuum level densities using the       1725 1451  391
 formulation of Gilbert and Cameron [Gi65] and pairing and shell  1725 1451  392
 parameters from the Cook [Co67] analysis.  Neutron and charged-  1725 1451  393
 particle transmission coefficients were obtained from the        1725 1451  394
 optical potentials, as discussed below.  Gamma-ray transmission  1725 1451  395
 coefficients were calculated using the Kopecky-Uhl model [Ko90]. 1725 1451  396
   Calculations were performed for all significant reactions      1725 1451  397
 producing neutrons, protons, deuterons, alpha particles, and     1725 1451  398
 gamma rays for incident neutrons between 100 keV and 20 MeV.     1725 1451  399
   The angular distribution systematics by Kalbach [Ka88] were    1725 1451  400
 used to describe the angular distributions for all continuum     1725 1451  401
 particles.                                                       1725 1451  402
                                                                  1725 1451  403
   OPTICAL MODEL POTENTIALS.  For incident and exiting neutrons,  1725 1451  404
 a phenomenological optical model potential by Arthur and Young   1725 1451  405
 [Ar80], based on an analysis of n + Fe and p + Fe reactions, was 1725 1451  406
 utilized for the neutron transmission coefficients, whereas the  1725 1451  407
 potential of Watanabe [Wa94] was used to calculate the neutron   1725 1451  408
 total cross section.  The two differenct potentials were used    1725 1451  409
 because on the one hand the Watanabe potential agreed well with  1725 1451  410
 total cross section measurements, whereas the Arthur potential   1725 1451  411
 appears to give a better reaction cross section, which is very   1725 1451  412
 important for Hauser-Feshbach calculations.  The SCAT2 optical   1725 1451  413
 model code [Be92] was used for all calculations.                 1725 1451  414
   To obtain transmission coefficients for charged particle       1725 1451  415
 reactions, the Beccetti-Greenlees potential [Be71] was used for  1725 1451  416
 protons, the Perey and Perey [Pe63] potential for deuterons, the 1725 1451  417
 Beccetti-Greenlees potential [Be69] for tritons,and the          1725 1451  418
 potential of MacFadden [Mc66] was used for alpha particles.      1725 1451  419
                                                                  1725 1451  420
   DIRECT REACTIONS:  Energy-dependent cross sections of          1725 1451  421
 inelastic neutrons from 35Cl(n,n') direct reactions were         1725 1451  422
 obtained using DWUCK calculations and deformation parameters     1725 1451  423
 estimated from systematics using the compilations of Raman et    1725 1451  424
 al. [Ra89] and Spear [Sp89].                                     1725 1451  425
                                                                  1725 1451  426
 EVALUATED DATA                                                   1725 1451  427
                                                                  1725 1451  428
   CALCULATIONAL RESULTS.  The MF=3 cross sections and MF=6       1725 1451  429
 energy/angle distributions that are based completely on          1725 1451  430
 calculations are:                                                1725 1451  431
                                                                  1725 1451  432
        MT =  16:     (n,2n) Reaction                             1725 1451  433
        MT =  22:     (n,nalpha) Reaction                         1725 1451  434
        MT =  28:     (n,np) Reaction                             1725 1451  435
        MT =  32:     (n,nd) Reaction                             1725 1451  436
        MT =  51-80:  (n,n') Discrete Level Reactions             1725 1451  437
        MT =  91:     (n,n') Continuum Reaction                   1725 1451  438
        MT = 103:     (n,p) Reaction                              1725 1451  439
        MT = 104:     (n,d) Reaction                              1725 1451  440
        MT = 105:     (n,t) Reaction                              1725 1451  441
        MT = 107:     (n,alpha) Reaction                          1725 1451  442
        MT = 111:     (n,2p) Reaction                             1725 1451  443
        MT = 112:     (n,palpha) Reaction                         1725 1451  444
        MT = 600-619: (n,p) Discrete Level Reactions              1725 1451  445
        MT = 649:     (n,p) Continuum Reaction                    1725 1451  446
        MT = 650-669: (n,d) Discrete Level Reactions              1725 1451  447
        MT = 699:     (n,d) Continuum Reaction                    1725 1451  448
        MT = 700-730: (n,t) Discrete Level Reactions              1725 1451  449
        MT = 749:     (n,t) Continuum Reaction                    1725 1451  450
        MT = 800-824: (n,alpha) Discrete Level Reactions          1725 1451  451
        MT = 849:     (n,alpha) Continuum Reaction                1725 1451  452
                                                                  1725 1451  453
   Additionally, (n,gamma) cross sections (multiplicities)        1725 1451  454
 included in MF=12 and energy distributions in MF=15 are taken    1725 1451  455
 from the GNASH calculations.  In addition to the usual Hauser-   1725 1451  456
 Feshbach cross section component obtained using the Kopecky-Uhl  1725 1451  457
 gamma-ray strength function, a semidirect component is included  1725 1451  458
 in GNASH that enhances the cross section and hardens the         1725 1451  459
 spectrum near En = 10-18 MeV.                                    1725 1451  460
   Discrete (n,n') cross sections and angular distributions were  1725 1451  461
 calculated using a combination of the GNASH and COMNUC [Du70]    1725 1451  462
 codes for compound nucleus reactions.  Preequilibrium components 1725 1451  463
 were included in the (n,n') reactions at higher energies to      1725 1451  464
 approximate direct reaction effects (in an average manner) for   1725 1451  465
 discrete states not included in the DWUCK calculations.  Cross   1725 1451  466
 sections for a total of 30 excited states are included for 35Cl. 1725 1451  467
 Discrete gamma-ray cross sections that exceed 1 mb are included  1725 1451  468
 explicitly in the MF=6 distributions for all reactions.  The     1725 1451  469
 elastic angular distributions are included in in MF =4;  the     1725 1451  470
 (n,n') angular distributions and (n,n'gamma) multiplicities are  1725 1451  471
 included in MF=6.                                                1725 1451  472
   Cross sections (MF=3) for approximately 20 or more discrete    1725 1451  473
 states are included for charged-particle reactions.  Again,      1725 1451  474
 multiplicities for (n,x gamma) reactions are included in MF=6.   1725 1451  475
   Kalbach systematics (Ka88) are used to specify all continuum   1725 1451  476
 particle angular distributions in MF=6.  All continuum photon    1725 1451  477
 angular distributions are assumed isotropic.  Residual nucleus   1725 1451  478
 recoil energy spectra are included in MF=6 for all reactions.    1725 1451  479
                                                                  1725 1451  480
   USE OF EXPERIMENTAL DATA.  The available experimental data for 1725 1451  481
 35Cl is somewhat limited.  Data that were useful for the         1725 1451  482
 evaluation are measurements of the neutron total cross section   1725 1451  483
 for natural Cl; measurements of (n,gamma) cross sections for     1725 1451  484
 35Cl and 37Cl at lower energies;  and a few isolated             1725 1451  485
 measurements near 14 MeV of (n,2n) or (n,x) reactions leading to 1725 1451  486
 radioactive products.                                            1725 1451  487
   The only adjustment made to the calculated cross sections on   1725 1451  488
 the basis of the experimental data was to fine tune the          1725 1451  489
 normalization of the gamma-ray strength function calculations    1725 1451  490
 using the radiative capture measurement.  The final              1725 1451  491
 normalization that was determined is near the middle of the      1725 1451  492
 range of adjustment that we have established from systematics in 1725 1451  493
 other analyses.                                                  1725 1451  494
                                                                  1725 1451  495
